// The first stages toward a Radeon that takes commands (a Polaris 10,
// the RX 580: GFX 8, memory controller GMC 8.1, OSS 3.0): the card's
// engines read and described, the card's memory divided between the
// display and the driver, the GART turned on, the interrupt ring brought
// up. Each step is the study's (/tmp/brix-next/gpu-accel-kernel.verified.md
// section 8, built from Linux 6.6's amdgpu), with its self-test, its one
// line in the log, its way back and the cautions it names; nothing here
// uploads firmware, sends the SMC a message or starts an engine.
//
// THE STAGES (../engine_choice.mlx says which one a boot goes to: a digit
// held while the machine starts; none: stage 0):
// 0  S0  the card read and described: the SMC, the memory controller, the
//        GART's words, the interrupt ring's, every engine's halt words, the
//        ROM's numbers of the graphics block and its clocks, temperature
//        and fan, the nine firmware files this card takes. Nothing of the
//        card is written but three index words (the SMC's, the memory
//        controller's IO debug index, the queue selector), each put back
//        as it was found.
// 1  M1  the aperture divided: the display's part below D (the aperture
//        less the top 64 MiB), the driver's above it; the driver's part
//        written with a pattern, read back and cleared, and one word beyond
//        the aperture through the index window, put back.
//    M2  the GART on: 256 MiB at the place Linux's rule gives, its table in
//        the driver's part, the dma piece mapped at its first 2048 entries;
//        what Linux's memory start writes before it (the memory controller's
//        golden words, the system aperture's default page, the AGP words,
//        the host path's two words) where it differs, then the twenty
//        steps of gmc_v8_0_gart_enable.
// 2  I1  the interrupt ring: 64 KiB of the system's memory, its read
//        pointer by register (no doorbell), the card's interrupt routed as a
//        message; it has to stay empty for a second.
//
// AROUND EVERY STEP (the study's 8.2):
// - every register a step writes is read first and kept (`Kept`, in the
//   order written); a step that fails puts its own words back, last first,
//   and no later step runs; the steps before it stay up;
// - every wait has its end (Linux's counts: 100 ms for the memory
//   controller's idle and a queue's dequeue);
// - the picture (the controller that shows the kernel's console): its
//   frames counted, the memory interface's underflow bit, a sum over the
//   picture's memory with the log's lines kept off the screen meanwhile,
//   before and after; a rate off by more than 1 %, an underflow that was
//   not there, a changed sum fail the step even when its own test passed.
//   On a card that shows no picture of the kernel's the watch has nothing
//   to watch and says so;
// - the card alive afterwards: CONFIG_MEMSIZE still what it was, the
//   scratch register takes a value (put back), the status words said;
// - no register of the display's is written by any step (the study's M1
//   would set VGA_HDP_CONTROL's and VGA_RENDER_CONTROL's bits where they
//   differ from Linux's: they are read and said instead), and the hot plug
//   tick is kept off the card around each step by gpu.mlx.
//
// A CARD FOUND RUNNING (the study's 8.7): when S0 finds an interrupt ring,
// a copy engine, a compute queue or the command processors on (a start of
// brixOS before this one, without the card losing power), a boot that goes
// beyond stage 0 halts them by registers alone before anything else; the
// GART and a running SMC are taken over as they are. A boot that stays at
// stage 0 says what it found and writes nothing. `stop` is the way down:
// the interrupt ring off (the GART stays on: nothing reads through it).
//
// The registers and their fields are ../registers' modules, generated from
// Linux 6.6's headers (gmc_8_1, oss_3_0, bif_5_0, gfx_8_0, smu_7_1_3, and
// dce_11_2 for the display's words that are only read): no register's
// number is typed here. The other values and the orders are Linux's files'
// (gmc_v8_0.c, amdgpu_gmc.c, tonga_ih.c, amdgpu_ih.c, vi.c, amdgpu_device.c,
// gfx_v8_0.c, sdma_v3_0.c, smu7_smumgr.c, polaris10_smumgr.c, amdgpu_cgs.c,
// amdgpu_ucode.h, amdgpu_vm.h, amdgpu.h, atombios.h): tools/
// check_gpu_registers.py holds them. The card is reached through `Hands`
// (gpu.mlx lends the real one; tests/support/engine_card.mlx is a card
// that is a table, for tests 493 and 494).
const scanout = @import("./gpu_display.mlx")
const atom = @import("./atom.mlx")
const videorom = @import("./gpu_rom.mlx")

